pd.from_dummies() in Pandas

pd.from_dummies() does the opposite of pd.get_dummies().

get_dummies() → categorical value → dummy columns
------------


from_dummies() → dummy columns → categorical value
-------------


Example

First, create dummy columns:

import pandas as pd

df = pd.DataFrame({
    "service": ["Facial", "Haircut", "Manicure", "Facial"]
})

dummy = pd.get_dummies(df["service"], dtype=int)

print(dummy)

Output:

| Facial | Haircut | Manicure |
| -----: | ------: | -------: |
|      1 |       0 |        0 |
|      0 |       1 |        0 |
|      0 |       0 |        1 |
|      1 |       0 |        0 |


====

Now convert them back:

original = pd.from_dummies(dummy)

Output:
------

|          |
| -------- |
| Facial   |
| Haircut  |
| Manicure |
| Facial   |


====

💇 Beauty parlour example
------------------------

Original data
     ↓
"Facial"
"Haircut"
"Manicure"
     ↓
pd.get_dummies()
     ↓
Facial  Haircut  Manicure
  1       0        0
  0       1        0
  0       0        1
     ↓
pd.from_dummies()
     ↓
"Facial"
"Haircut"
"Manicure"


=====

🧠 Easy memory
pd.get_dummies()

Encode → Text → 0/1

pd.from_dummies()

Decode → 0/1 → Text

So simply remember:

get_dummies() = Convert to dummy variables
from_dummies() = Convert dummy variables back

====






